---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/attentive-alternative';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives/attentive-alternative');
const desc = description(site, 'alternatives/attentive-alternative');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When should we stay?",
    "answer": "Stay when the existing channels, reply service, event handling and total cost satisfy the documented operating requirements. Compare a specific repair with the disruption and service replacement cost of migration."
  },
  {
    "question": "What must a replacement include?",
    "answer": "Document sending identity, permission evidence, event mapping, queue ownership, reply coverage and escalation responsibilities. Obtain account-specific confirmation of transfer and managed-service scope."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is there a universal migration schedule?",
    "answer": "No. Authorize cutover after identity, permission, event, queue and service acceptance checks pass. Set measurement duration using eligible volume rather than a fixed calendar promise."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does moving email authorize SMS?",
    "answer": "No. Preserve and validate permission for the relevant channel and purpose; exclude unresolved records from that channel\u2019s sends."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-16 container">
<a href="/alternatives" class="link">← All alternatives</a>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-10">
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Attentive alternatives: replace the operating service as well as the sender</h1>
<h2>Start with the scope you actually use</h2>
<p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.attentive.com/pricing">Attentive’s pricing page</a> describes SMS and email, onboarding support and full-service setup, with pricing shaped by volume, subscribers, channels and selected AI products. <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4460351735060-Get-started-with-Attentive-Concierge">Concierge documentation</a> describes agent replies configured around the brand’s voice. These can be valuable services for a lean retention team. Keep Attentive in the evaluation if it meets your operating requirements; a new dashboard alone does not replace the people handling replies or maintaining journeys.</p>
<h2>Write a procurement acceptance record</h2>
<p>Inventory channels, sending numbers and domains, forms, event sources, audiences, active journeys, scheduled sends and reply destinations. Record which work is done by your staff, an agency or the supplier. Ask both the incumbent and a candidate to price the same scope: implementation, campaign production, data repair, replies, escalation coverage and ongoing optimization. Confirm which managed tasks are included in your actual agreement. List the named owner and acceptance evidence for each task rather than assuming every account includes every service.</p>
<h2>Separate identity, events and permission</h2>
<p>Ask the proposed supplier to explain number transfer eligibility, sender registration, domain authentication and reply routing before setting a cutover date. Preserve source identifiers and available channel, purpose, source and timestamp evidence with unsubscribe and suppression history. Reconcile sendable and excluded records separately; an email subscription does not establish SMS permission. Keep uncertain records out of affected sends. Export access and transfer eligibility need account-specific confirmation.</p>
<h2>Prove the cart state before authorizing sends</h2>
<p>These are proposed acceptance exercises, not observed results. In a test audience, create a cart, update its variant, complete a purchase, replay the cart event and unsubscribe while a reminder is queued. Record source event IDs, destination IDs, timestamps and the resulting eligibility decision. Require deduplication and a purchase exit before the queued reminder can send. Set the tolerated event delay from the store’s recovery policy and verify it; a generic fifteen-minute claim is not evidence.</p>
<h2>Hand over replies and queue ownership</h2>
<p>Send a sizing question and an order-support question through the proposed reply route. Have the service owner demonstrate the approved answer, escalation destination and access to relevant order context. Document coverage and what happens outside it. Freeze changes to the old journey, reconcile pending reminders and assign one sender for each journey before enabling the replacement. A rollback must pause the new queue and reconcile sends already made before restoring the old one; blindly reenabling both creates collisions.</p>
<h2>Choose the candidate around the missing work</h2>
<p>For SMS procurement, use <a href="/alternatives/postscript-alternative">the Postscript evaluation</a> to frame a channel-specific trial; for email and event orchestration, use <a href="/alternatives/klaviyo-alternative">the Klaviyo evaluation</a> to prepare questions. These are research paths, not a ranking or a promise of service parity. Require a current demonstration and quote against the acceptance record. Keeping Attentive and repairing an event mapping or service handoff may cost less than rebuilding a working program.</p>
<h2>Retain useful recovery cases without arbitrary timing</h2>
<p>For mobile distraction, verify the restored cart link and a no-discount reminder. For financing abandonment, link to accurate payment information without claiming approval. For an unavailable variant, withhold the stale item and require approval for substitutes. For shipping shock, show destination-specific charges. For gift sizing, route to a size guide or staffed reply. For minimum-order quantities, show the actual minimum. For stacked coupons, test eligibility and combined discount behavior. Include a repeat purchaser in each relevant exclusion check; choose message timing and frequency from store behavior and service capacity, then evaluate them.</p>
<h2>Keep goods cost fixed when costing an offer</h2>
<p>Illustrative economics: a $757 order at 56% gross margin has $333.08 goods cost and $423.92 gross profit. A 20% selling-price discount leaves $605.60 revenue and $272.52 gross profit, reducing profit by $151.40 before messaging, shipping, refunds and platform fees. Compare incremental contribution against an eligible control group with enough volume and a declared measurement window. Attributed recovery revenue alone cannot establish lift.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</section>
</BaseLayout>
